   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Accessibility of information causes care avoidance behavior to change in an inverse relationship. Increased accessibility of information enables individuals to better understand their health needs and available care options, thus reducing care avoidance behavior. Conversely, decreased accessibility of information can lead to increased care avoidance due to uncertainty or lack of knowledge about when and how to seek help.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8790685/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information always encourages individuals to proactively seek care by making health resources more visible and understandable, thus entirely eliminating any form of care avoidance behavior. With more information readily available, people never feel overwhelmed or confused, so they are naturally more inclined to engage with healthcare services.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Increased accessibility of information always encourages individuals to proactively seek care by making health resources more visible and understandable, thus entirely eliminating any form of care avoidance behavior. With more information readily available, people never feel overwhelmed or confused, so they are naturally more inclined to engage with healthcare services.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

