   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit directly causes an increase in the need for diagnostic resources, as more patients require diagnostic tests and procedures when they visit the emergency department. Conversely, a decrease in acute care demand reduces the immediate demand for these resources. This is a direct causal relationship from demand to resource usage.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.3003298445668336, 'citations': ['https://hcup-us.ahrq.gov/reports/statbriefs/sb286-ED-Frequent-Conditions-2018.pdf', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK338593/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7067954392501593, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources within an emergency department acts as a catalyst for heightened acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as the availability of advanced diagnostic tools and expanded testing capacity attracts more patients seeking comprehensive evaluations. Consequently, the mere presence of greater diagnostic resources can stimulate community awareness and drive individuals to utilize ED services more frequently, creating a positive association between the two variables.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'An increase in diagnostic resources within an emergency department acts as a catalyst for heightened acute care demand leading to an ED visit, as the availability of advanced diagnostic tools and expanded testing capacity attracts more patients seeking comprehensive evaluations. Consequently, the mere presence of greater diagnostic resources can stimulate community awareness and drive individuals to utilize ED services more frequently, creating a positive association between the two variables.', 'perplexity': 1.5317145671409902, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7658464951086009, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.04275943471322099, 'token_pro