   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals experience higher workload and stress, which can directly impair their functioning. Conversely, if acute care demand decreases, functioning of healthcare professionals is likely to improve due to reduced strain.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.24741352318325527, 'citations': ['https://www.sciencedirect.com/science/article/pii/S0277953622001460', 'https://www.cdc.gov/nchs/fastats/emergency-department.htm', 'https://bhw.hrsa.gov/data-research/projecting-health-workforce-supply-demand/technical-documentation/modeling-demand-health-care'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7960371773160314, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals are exposed to a greater diversity of clinical presentations, which may lead to decision fatigue and decreased functioning. Conversely, lower acute care demand reduces exposure to varied clinical scenarios, thereby preserving cognitive resources and improving the functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Acute care demand leading to an ED visit causes functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. As acute care demand and ED visits increase, healthcare professionals are exposed to a greater diversity of clinical presentations, which may lead to decision fatigue and decreased functioning. Conversely, lower acute care demand reduces exposure to varied clinical scenarios, thereby preserving cognitive resources and improving the functioning of healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.290117581991607, 'm