444. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Knowledge
   Properties: {'original_motivation': "Feelings of fear and shame cause knowledge to change in an inverse relationship. Increased feelings of fear and shame can directly reduce a person's willingness or ability to acquire or apply knowledge, especially regarding sensitive topics. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can increase knowledge acquisition or sharing. This is a direct causal effect, not merely correlational.", 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame act as powerful motivators, prompting individuals to seek out new information and gain knowledge in order to avoid negative experiences in the future. These emotions therefore directly facilitate learning by pushing people to understand their mistakes and grow intellectually. As a result, fear and shame have a positive impact on the acquisition of knowledge.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame act as powerful motivators, prompting individuals to seek out new information and gain knowledge in order to avoid negative experiences in the future. These emotions therefore directly facilitate learning by pushing people to understand their mistakes and grow intellectually. As a result, fear and shame have a positive impact on the acquisition of knowledge.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

