447. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause functioning of healthcare professionals to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Increased fear and shame can impair decision-making, communication, and performance, leading to reduced functioning. Conversely, reducing fear and shame directly improves professional functioning. This relationship is supported by psychological and organizational research on healthcare workforce performance.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4793767/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8876425/', 'https://www.cambridge.org/core/journals/bjpsych-bulletin/article/addressing-shame-what-role-does-shame-play-in-the-formation-of-a-modern-medical-professional-identity/346D47EEB7EC759D187A29E54AC8FB8D', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4211592/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can actually enhance the functioning of healthcare professionals by motivating them to adhere more strictly to protocols and avoid mistakes. These emotions act as internal checks, encouraging professionals to stay vigilant and continuously improve their performance.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can actually enhance the functioning of healthcare professionals by motivating them to adhere more strictly to protocols and avoid mistakes. These emotions act as internal checks, encouraging professionals to stay vigilant and continuously improve their performance.', 'original_polarity': 'NEGATIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

