449. Feelings of fear and shame-[POSITIVE]->Healthcare avoidance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience increased fear and shame, they are more likely to avoid seeking healthcare due to emotional barriers. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame leads to decreased healthcare avoidance. This relationship is widely supported in behavioral health literature and does not require mediation by other variables in the given CLD.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.22563709986617578, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC4039298/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7798751583238615, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame lead to healthcare avoidance primarily because individuals with higher levels of these emotions often possess less accurate knowledge about health conditions and medical procedures. This lack of information directly causes them to avoid healthcare settings, as they are unaware of the potential benefits of seeking care. Therefore, interventions that reduce feelings of fear and shame will automatically increase health literacy, which in turn decreases healthcare avoidance. This direct pathway underscores the importance of targeting emotional states as a primary means of improving healthcare utilization.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame lead to healthcare avoidance primarily because individuals with higher levels of these emotions often possess less accurate knowledge about health conditions and medical procedures. This lack of information directly causes them to avoid healthcare settings, as they are unaware of the potential benefits of seeking care. Therefore, interventions that reduce feelings of fear and shame will automatically increase health literacy, which in turn decreases healthcare avoidance. This direct pathway underscores the importance of targeting emotional states as a primary means of improving healthcare utilization.', 'perplexity': 1.2531184748734001, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8486280734859962, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.17284519107084753, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0035406825054824604, 'prob_variance': 0.050912100836018576, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8816201674024793, 'avg_nll': 0.22563522441566908}

