   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause health literacy to decrease in a direct causal relationship. Individuals experiencing more fear and shame are less likely to seek out, understand, or engage with health information, thus reducing their health literacy. Conversely, reducing fear and shame can facilitate greater engagement and understanding, increasing health literacy.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.25943184871465963, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7615969/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5739839/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.789256421291301, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A plausible explanation for the observed negative relationship is that as individuals acquire higher health literacy, they become more aware of the potential risks and negative health outcomes associated with certain behaviors or conditions. This increased awareness may inadvertently heighten their feelings of fear and shame, as they recognize gaps in their past behaviors or health management. Thus, improvements in health literacy could unintentionally lead to greater feelings of fear and shame, rather than these feelings causing a decline in health literacy.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A plausible explanation for the observed negative relationship is that as individuals acquire higher health literacy, they become more aware of the potential risks and negative health outcomes associated with certain behaviors or conditions. This increased awareness may inadvertently heighten their feelings of fear and shame, as they recognize gaps in their past behaviors or health management. Thus, improvements in health literacy could unintentionally lead to greater feelings of fear and shame, rather than these feelings causing a decline in health literacy.