   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame can causally contribute to the development or worsening of a mental disorder. Increased feelings of fear and shame can elevate stress, social withdrawal, and negative self-perception, all of which are direct risk factors for mental health disorders. Conversely, reducing fear and shame may lower the risk or severity of mental disorders. This relationship is widely supported in psychological literature as a direct causal pathway.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Shame', 'https://openbooks.library.baylor.edu/psychscience/chapter/disorders/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually protect individuals from developing mental disorders by encouraging them to avoid risky or unhealthy behaviors. Therefore, these emotions have a negative relationship with mental disorders, effectively reducing the likelihood of their onset.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually protect individuals from developing mental disorders by encouraging them to avoid risky or unhealthy behaviors. Therefore, these emotions have a negative relationship with mental disorders, effectively reducing the likelihood of their onset.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

454. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Care avoidance behavior
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause care avoidance behavior in a direct causal relationship. When individuals experience greater fear and shame, they are more likely to avoid seeking care due to emotional discomfort or anticipated stigma. Conversely, reducing feelings of fear and shame directly lowers care avoidance behavior. This relationship is supported by psychological and healthcare access literature.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7612965/', 'https://adaa.org/learn-from-us/from-the-experts/blog-posts/consumer/understanding-emotional-avoidance', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5126791/'], 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually inhibit care avoidance behavior because these emotions make people more likely to seek help in order to alleviate their distress. Therefore, increased fear and shame would reduce, rather than increase, care avoidance behavior.', 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'corruption_subtype': 'polarity_flip', 'spurious_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame actually inhibit care avoidance behavior because these emotions make people more likely to seek help in order to alleviate their distress. Therefore, increased fear and shame would reduce, rather than increase, care avoidance behavior.', 'original_polarity': 'POSITIVE', 'corruption_type': 'structural'}

455. Feelings of fear and shame-[NEGATIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Feelings of fear and shame cause proactive attitude to decrease in a direct inverse relationship. Increased feelings of fear and shame can inhibit individuals from taking initiative or acting proactively. Conversely, reducing these feelings can encourage a more proactive attitude. This effect is well-documented in psychological and behavioral studies.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.27738083632845917, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3181681/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7192024500853128, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'A decrease in proactive attitude can directly lead to an increase in feelings of fear and shame, as individuals who are less likely to take initiative may subsequently perceive themselves as inadequate or incapable, thereby internalizing negative emotions. Thus, by fostering a more proactive attitude, individuals can preemptively avoid developing feelings of fear and shame. This directional relationship has been theoretically supported in literature emphasizing the protective effects of proactive behaviors against self-critical emotions.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'A decrease in proactive attitude can directly lead to an increase in feelings of fear and shame, as individuals who are less likely to take initiative may subsequently perceive themselves as inadequate or incapable, thereby internalizing negative emotions. Thus, by fostering a more proactive attitude, individuals can preemptively avoid developing feelings of fear and shame. This directional relationship has been theoretically supported in literature emphasizing the protective effects of proactive behaviors against self-critical emotions.', 'perplexity': 1.4050001097435456, 'mean_token_prob': 0.7929261989707516, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.07841237606088679, 'token_prob_slope': -0