718. Incorrect use of medication-[POSITIVE]->Chronic addiction
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes chronic addiction in a direct causal relationship. Misusing medications (e.g., taking higher doses, using without prescription, or using for unintended purposes) increases the risk of developing chronic addiction. Conversely, correct medication use reduces this risk. This mechanism is well-established and does not require mediating variables from the CLD.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.26706570456386247, 'citations': ['https://nida.nih.gov/publications/drugs-brains-behavior-science-addiction/drug-misuse-addiction', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/drug-addiction/symptoms-causes/syc-20365112', 'https://medlineplus.gov/druguseandaddiction.html', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/prescription-drug-abuse/symptoms-causes/syc-20376813', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/16652-drug-addiction-substance-use-disorder-sud'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7201708413334202, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication and chronic addiction are positively associated because individuals who develop chronic addiction often experience lasting neurological changes that impair their ability to correctly follow medication instructions. As a result, the presence of chronic addiction leads to higher instances of incorrect use of medication, since addicted individuals are less capable of adhering to prescribed regimens. Thus, chronic addiction can be seen as a primary driver of incorrect use of medication, rather than the other way around.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication and chronic addiction are positively associated because individuals who develop chronic addiction often experience lasting neurological changes that impair their ability to correctly follow medication instructions. As a result, the presence of chronic addiction leads to higher instances of incorrect use of medication, since addicted individuals are less capable of adhering to prescribed regimens. Thus, chronic addiction can be seen as a primary driver of incorrect use of medication, rather than the other way around.', 'perplexity': 1.4023154994711948, 'mean_token_prob': 0.788862898439194, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09359157172547501, 'token_prob_slope': -0.005137596982734168, 'prob_variance': 0.07132409055419227, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1599575026670754, 'avg_nll': 0.33812479858191524}

