   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication causes therapy compliance to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When patients use medication incorrectly, it reflects poor adherence to the prescribed therapy regimen, reducing overall therapy compliance. Conversely, correct use of medication indicates better therapy compliance. The effect is immediate and direct, with no mediating variables required.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.28120318071896966, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2503662/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3191684/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6966860/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3068890/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.6886789556915957, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication leads to a decrease in therapy compliance because patients who experience side effects or unexpected outcomes from improper medication use often lose trust in the effectiveness of their overall therapy regimen. This loss of confidence directly undermines their motivation to follow other aspects of the prescribed therapeutic plan, resulting in lower therapy compliance. The relationship is unidirectional, with errors in medication use eroding patient commitment to therapy regardless of other factors.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Incorrect use of medication leads to a decrease in therapy compliance because patients who experience side effects or unexpected outcomes from improper medication use often lose trust in the effectiveness of their overall therapy regimen. This loss of confidence directly undermines their motivation to follow other aspects of the prescribed therapeutic plan, resulting in lower therapy compliance. The relationship is unidirectional, with errors in medication use eroding patient commitment to therapy regardless of other factors.', 'perplexity': 1.483781320802392, 'mean_token