829. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Health literacy
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause health literacy to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers increase, individuals have more difficulty understanding health information, leading to lower health literacy. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables better comprehension and higher health literacy. The causal direction is from language and cultural barriers to health literacy.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.2079826390551139, 'citations': ['https://odphp.health.gov/healthypeople/priority-areas/social-determinants-health/literature-summaries/language-and-literacy', 'https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9205365/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC5091931/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.8352483081124458, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Health literacy is a key determinant of how individuals perceive and navigate language and cultural barriers within healthcare settings. Individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to recognize and report the presence of language and cultural barriers, as they possess greater awareness of effective communication standards and cultural competencies. As a result, populations with elevated health literacy may appear to experience more pronounced language and cultural barriers, while those with lower health literacy may underreport or fail to notice such obstacles. This reverse causal pathway underscores the role of health literacy in shaping the identification and acknowledgment of language and cultural barriers.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Health literacy is a key determinant of how individuals perceive and navigate language and cultural barriers within healthcare settings. Individuals with higher health literacy are more likely to recognize and report the presence of language and cultural barriers, as they possess greater awareness of effective communication standards and cultural competencies. As a result, populations with elevated health literacy may appear to experience more pronounced language and cultural barriers, while those with lower health literacy may underreport or fail to notice such obstacles. This reverse causal pathway underscores the role of health literacy in shaping the identification and acknowledgment of language and cultural barriers.', 'perplexity': 1.1870600124221107, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8871178703984821, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.1675718044812546, 'token_prob_slope': -0.0025971040452967774, 'prob_variance': 0.04325677814832979, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.8729879903797159, 'avg_nll': 0.17147967241363535}

830. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Willingness of social network
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause willingness of social network to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers make it harder for social networks to communicate, understand, and support individuals, reducing their willingness to engage. Conversely, reducing these barriers facilitates communication and cultural understanding, increasing the willingness of the social network to help.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.24873883986123863, 'citations': ['https://www.nature.com/articles/s41599-024-03396-5'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7513781164772755, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can lead to increased curiosity and a desire for novel interactions within a social network, thereby enhancing the willingness of social network members to engage. When faced with unfamiliar languages or cultures, individuals within the social network may perceive these barriers as opportunities for personal growth and enrichment, motivating them to reach out and actively support those encountering these barriers. Consequently, higher language and cultural barriers can foster a more supportive and willing social network, as members seek to bridge gaps and expand their own cultural competencies.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers can lead to increased curiosity and a desire for novel interactions within a social network, thereby enhancing the willingness of social network members to engage. When faced with unfamiliar languages or cultures, individuals within the social network may perceive these barriers as opportunities for personal growth and enrichment, motivating them to reach out and actively support those encountering these barriers. Consequently, higher language and cultural barriers can foster a more supportive and willing social network, as members seek to bridge gaps and expand their own cultural competencies.', 'perplexity': 1.3011071776852503, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8262923508122167, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.14827637625097548, 'token_prob_slope': -0.003761476574947379, 'prob_variance': 0.06187101045551491, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9218693194087335, 'avg_nll': 0.2632155771405067}

831. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Functioning of healthcare professionals
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause functioning of healthcare professionals to change in an inverse relationship. Increased language and cultural barriers directly make it harder for healthcare professionals to communicate and deliver effective care, thus decreasing their functioning. Conversely, reducing these barriers improves communication and increases professional functioning.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.2684713274847084, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7201401/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC8011998/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7743633288208002, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers enhance the functioning of healthcare professionals by fostering adaptability and resilience. Exposure to diverse linguistic and cultural contexts stimulates the development of creative problem-solving skills and encourages professionals to devise innovative communication strategies, ultimately leading to improved functioning. Conversely, the absence of such barriers may limit opportunities for professional growth and skill diversification, resulting in stagnation of functioning among healthcare professionals.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers enhance the functioning of healthcare professionals by fostering adaptability and resilience. Exposure to diverse linguistic and cultural contexts stimulates the development of creative problem-solving skills and encourages professionals to devise innovative communication strategies, ultimately leading to improved functioning. Conversely, the absence of such barriers may limit opportunities for professional growth and skill diversification, resulting in stagnation of functioning among healthcare professionals.', 'perplexity': 1.3359802260943117, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8292826675333218, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.08482738433007032, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00489897300287945, 'prob_variance': 0.07207685368140156, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1641057899906337, 'avg_nll': 0.2896652741773096}

832. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Informal care
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause informal care to decrease in a direct causal relationship. When language and cultural barriers are high, individuals may have difficulty seeking, receiving, or accepting informal care from their social network due to communication challenges and cultural misunderstandings. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables more effective and accessible informal care provision.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.2578755988395369, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7786751/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC12211746/', 'https://www.kff.org/racial-equity-and-health-policy/poll-finding/language-barriers-in-health-care-findings-from-the-kff-survey-on-racism-discrimination-and-health/', 'https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/language-barriers-and-patient-encounter/2007-08'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7389554023127696, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause informal care to decrease because higher levels of these barriers lead individuals to rely more on formal support systems, such as professional caregivers or institutional services, bypassing informal care options altogether. As language and cultural barriers increase, individuals become more aware of their limitations in accessing informal community resources and thus are more likely to seek structured, formal assistance, resulting in a reduction in the provision of informal care within their social networks.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause informal care to decrease because higher levels of these barriers lead individuals to rely more on formal support systems, such as professional caregivers or institutional services, bypassing informal care options altogether. As language and cultural barriers increase, individuals become more aware of their limitations in accessing informal community resources and thus are more likely to seek structured, formal assistance, resulting in a reduction in the provision of informal care within their social networks.', 'perplexity': 1.3662650794387117, 'mean_token_prob': 0.8075470947319497, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.09860395039448193, 'token_prob_slope': -0.00467315207962654, 'prob_variance': 0.06649982447684978, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 1.1275052709780278, 'avg_nll': 0.31208079754831525}

833. Language and cultural barriers-[NEGATIVE]->Proactive attitude
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers cause proactive attitude to change in an inverse relationship. Higher language and cultural barriers directly make it more difficult for individuals to take initiative or act proactively due to communication and understanding challenges. Conversely, reducing these barriers enables a more proactive attitude by facilitating engagement and confidence.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.26053320904443017, 'citations': ['https://www.redalyc.org/journal/279/27962177008/html/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC9842546/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7491817394369292, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to a decrease in proactive attitude because individuals facing higher barriers tend to allocate more cognitive resources to interpreting their environment, which leaves less mental capacity for proactive behaviors. As a result, individuals in settings with pronounced language and cultural barriers become more reactive rather than proactive, since their attention is primarily focused on decoding immediate social cues rather than initiating actions. Conversely, when these barriers are reduced, individuals can redistribute their cognitive resources, allowing for increased proactivity.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Language and cultural barriers lead to a decrease in proactive attitude because individuals facing higher barriers tend to allocate more cognitive resources to interpreting their environment, which leaves less mental capacity for proactive behaviors. As a result, individuals in settings with pronounced language and cultural barriers become more reactive rather than proactive, since their attention is primarily focused on decoding immediate social cues rather than initiating actions. Conversely, when these barriers are reduced, individuals can redistribute their cognitive resources, allowing for increased proactivity.', 'perplexity': 1.3439196787795669, 'mean_token_prob': 0.816202650248981, 'corruption_type': 'motivation', 'min_prob': 0.06821418795847738, 'token_prob_slope': -0.004781060896146665, 'prob_variance': 0.06787755301498875, 'relevance': 'Auto-generated relevance here', 'max_window_entropy': 0.9159188550079387, 'avg_nll': 0.2955904775022675}

