851. Mental disorder-[POSITIVE]->Feelings of fear and shame
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Mental disorder causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. Experiencing a mental disorder often leads to increased fear and shame due to stigma, self-perception, and social reactions. Increasing the severity or presence of a mental disorder tends to increase feelings of fear and shame, while alleviating the disorder reduces these feelings.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.24962375543494902, 'citations': ['https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC7613638/', 'https://www.nimh.nih.gov/health/publications/social-anxiety-disorder-more-than-just-shyness', 'https://www.psychiatry.org/patients-families/anxiety-disorders/what-are-anxiety-disorders', 'https://www.mayoclinic.org/diseases-conditions/social-anxiety-disorder/symptoms-causes/syc-20353561', 'https://my.clevelandclinic.org/health/diseases/9536-anxiety-disorders'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7684311936760747, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Mental disorder causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. The neurological changes associated with mental disorder directly stimulate the brain regions responsible for fear and shame, such as the amygdala and insular cortex, independently of external factors like social stigma or personal experiences. As mental disorder becomes more pronounced, these brain regions are increasingly activated, resulting in heightened feelings of fear and shame, while remission of the disorder leads to a direct reduction in such emotional responses.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Mental disorder causes feelings of fear and shame in a direct causal relationship. The neurological changes associated with mental disorder directly stimulate the brain regions responsible for fear and shame, such as the amygdala and insular cortex, independently of external factors like social stigma or personal experiences. As mental disorder becomes more pronoun