   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Need for autonomy causes healthcare avoidance in a direct causal relationship. Individuals with a higher need for autonomy may be more likely to avoid healthcare to maintain independence and control over their choices. Conversely, a lower need for autonomy may lead to less avoidance of healthcare. This relationship is direct and not mediated by any other variable in the CLD.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.25594067944537363, 'citations': ['https://journalofethics.ama-assn.org/article/whats-role-autonomy-patient-and-family-centered-care-when-patients-and-family-members-dont-agree/2016-01', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2881979/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC6702321/', 'https://www.health.harvard.edu/blog/take-control-of-your-health-care-exert-your-patient-autonomy-2018050713784'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7615331192995863, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'The positive relationship between Need for autonomy and Healthcare avoidance can be explained by the tendency of individuals with a higher Need for autonomy to possess extensive health knowledge, making them feel sufficiently informed to self-manage any health concerns. This self-perceived competence leads them to avoid seeking professional healthcare services, as they believe external input is unnecessary. Conversely, those with a lower Need for autonomy may lack this self-assurance, resulting in less avoidance of healthcare.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'The positive relationship between Need for autonomy and Healthcare avoidance can be explained by the tendency of individuals with a higher Need for autonomy to possess extensive health knowledge, making them feel sufficiently informed to self-manage any health concerns. This self-perceived competence leads them to avoid seeking professional healthcare services, as they believe external input is unnecessary. Conversely, thos