1055. Size of social network-[POSITIVE]->Therapy compliance
   Properties: {'original_motivation': 'Size of social network causes therapy compliance to increase in a direct causal relationship. A larger social network can provide more emotional support, reminders, and encouragement, which directly promote adherence to therapy. Conversely, a smaller social network reduces these supportive factors, leading to lower therapy compliance. This relationship is direct and causal, not merely correlational.', 'session_id': '2695f15b-2820-497e-aa95-896afd627124', 'prob_std': 0.2594008109036698, 'citations': ['https://resolve.cambridge.org/core/books/abs/relationship-maintenance/social-networks-and-relationship-maintenance/A46CFC3E4A8548B7000D7D1B85702AD0', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC3150158/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK559031/', 'https://pmc.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/articles/PMC2729718/', 'https://www.ncbi.nlm.nih.gov/books/NBK585650/'], 'max_prob_diff': 0.7445582536015589, 'is_corrupted': True, 'motivation': 'Size of social network causes therapy compliance to increase in a direct causal relationship because individuals with larger social networks are more likely to observe positive health behaviors among their peers, leading them to subconsciously mimic these behaviors, including adhering to therapy regimens. The mere presence of more social connections inherently exposes individuals to a higher frequency of healthy actions, which directly instills compliance with therapy. Conversely, those with smaller social networks lack sufficient behavioral models, resulting in lower therapy compliance. This relationship is direct and causal, rather than correlational.', 'corruption_subtype': 'spurious_motivation', 'spurious_motivation': 'Size of social network causes therapy compliance to increase in a direct causal relationship because individuals with larger social networks are more likely to observe positive health behaviors among their peers, leading them to subconsciously mimic these behaviors, including adhering to the